1
00:00:00,730 --> 00:00:05,701
Today we're pleased to have on the show Alice Bussell from the Dolphin Conservation Trust.

2
00:00:05,701 --> 00:00:07,690
Tell us about the Trust, Alice.

3
00:00:07,690 --> 00:00:13,810
Well, obviously its purpose is to protect dolphins in seas all around the world.

4
00:00:13,810 --> 00:00:18,864
It tries to raise people's awareness of the problems these marine creatures are suffering

5
00:00:18,864 --> 00:00:21,030
because of pollution and other threats.

6
00:00:21,030 --> 00:00:23,340
It started ten years ago

7
00:00:23,340 --> 00:00:27,105
and it's one of the fastest growing animal charities in the country -

8
00:00:27,105 --> 00:00:31,750
although it's still fairly small compared with the big players in animal protection.

9
00:00:31,750 --> 00:00:35,600
We are particularly proud of the work we do in education

10
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:41,116
last year we visited a huge number of schools in different parts of the country,

11
00:00:41,116 --> 00:00:46,160
going round to talk to children and young people aged from five to eighteen.

12
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:50,340
In fact, about thirty-five per cent of our members are children.

13
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:54,120
The charity uses its money to support campaigns

14
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:58,900
for example, for changes in fishing policy and so forth.

15
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:03,907
It hopes soon to be able to employ its first full-time biologist -

16
00:01:03,907 --> 00:01:06,410
with dolphin expertise - to monitor populations.

17
00:01:06,410 --> 00:01:10,998
Of course, many people give their services on a voluntary basis

18
00:01:10,998 --> 00:01:16,420
and we now have volunteers working in observation, office work and other things.

19
00:01:16,420 --> 00:01:22,066
I should also tell you about the award we won from the Charity Commission last year -

20
00:01:22,066 --> 00:01:23,830
for our work in education.

21
00:01:23,830 --> 00:01:27,565
Although it's not meant an enormous amount of money for us,

22
00:01:27,565 --> 00:01:31,300
it has made our activities even more widely publicised and understood.

23
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:34,880
In the long term it may not bring in extra members

24
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:37,220
but we're hoping it'll have this effect.

25
00:01:37,220 --> 00:01:41,140
Is it possible to see dolphins in UK waters?

26
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:47,240
Yes. In several locations. And we have a big project in the east part of Scotland.

27
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:51,700
This has long been a haven for dolphins because it has very little shipping.

28
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:54,860
However, that may be about to change soon

29
00:01:54,860 --> 00:01:58,260
because oil companies want to increase exploration there.

30
00:01:58,260 --> 00:02:00,568
We're campaigning against this because,

31
00:02:00,568 --> 00:02:03,800
although there'll be little pollution from oil

32
00:02:03,880 --> 00:02:07,030
exploration creates a lot of underwater noise.

33
00:02:07,030 --> 00:02:10,360
It means the dolphins can't rest and socialise.

34
00:02:10,360 --> 00:02:15,150
This is how I became interested in dolphin conservation in the first place.

35
00:02:15,150 --> 00:02:19,910
I had never seen one and I hadn't been particularly interested in them at school.

36
00:02:19,910 --> 00:02:24,120
Then I came across this story about a family of dolphins

37
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:26,732
who had to leave their home in the Moray Firth

38
00:02:26,732 --> 00:02:31,280
because of the oil companies and about a child who campaigned to save them.

39
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:35,990
I couldn't put the book down - I was hooked.

40
00:02:35,990 --> 00:02:39,870
I'm sure our listeners will want to find out what they can do to help.

41
00:02:39,870 --> 00:02:44,550
You mentioned the 'Adopt a Dolphin' scheme. Can you tell us about that?

42
00:02:44,550 --> 00:02:49,040
Of course! People can choose one of our dolphins to sponsor.

43
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:52,470
They receive a picture of it and news updates.

44
00:02:52,470 --> 00:02:59,302
I'd like to tell you about four which are currently being adopted by our members:

45
00:02:59,302 --> 00:03:01,580
Moondancer, Echo, Kiwi and Samson.

46
00:03:01,580 --> 00:03:04,595
Unfortunately, Echo is being rather elusive this year

47
00:03:04,595 --> 00:03:08,040
and hasn't yet been sighted by our observers

48
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:11,380
but we remain optimistic that he'll be out there soon.

49
00:03:11,380 --> 00:03:16,456
All the others have been out in force - Samson and Moondancer are often photographed together

50
00:03:16,456 --> 00:03:22,885
but it is Kiwi who's our real character as she seems to love coming up close for the cameras

51
00:03:22,885 --> 00:03:25,930
and we've captured her on film hundreds of times.

52
00:03:25,930 --> 00:03:28,083
They all have their own personalities -

53
00:03:28,083 --> 00:03:32,749
Moondancer is very elegant and curves out and into the water very smoothly,

54
00:03:32,749 --> 00:03:35,940
whereas Samson has a lot of energy

55
00:03:36,220 --> 00:03:38,850
he's always leaping out of the water with great vigour.

56
00:03:38,850 --> 00:03:41,905
You'd probably expect him to be the youngest -

57
00:03:41,905 --> 00:03:44,520
he's not quite - that's Kiwi

58
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:48,780
but Samson's the latest of our dolphins to be chosen for the scheme.

59
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:53,310
Kiwi makes a lot of noise so we can often pick her out straightaway.

60
00:03:53,310 --> 00:03:57,127
Echo and Moondancer are noisy too, but Moondancer's easy to find

61
00:03:57,127 --> 00:04:00,960
because she has a particularly large fin on her back

62
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:02,680
which makes her easy to identify.

63
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:05,220
So, yes, they're all very different...

64
00:04:05,220 --> 00:04:07,560
Well, they sound a fascinating group...
